@anon.bcaf8a6642ef434db28a340da408f3d7.1760 = private unnamed_addr constant [978 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for redundant `Literal[None]` annotations.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AWhile `Literal[None]` is a valid type annotation, it is semantically equivalent to `None`.\0APrefer `None` over `Literal[None]` for both consistency and readability.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom typing import Literal\0A\0ALiteral[None]\0ALiteral[1, 2, 3, \22foo\22, 5, None]\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom typing import Literal\0A\0ANone\0ALiteral[1, 2, 3, \22foo\22, 5] | None\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety and availability\0AThis rule's fix is marked as safe unless the literal contains comments.\0A\0AThere is currently no fix available when applying the fix would lead to\0Aa `TypeError` from an expression of the form `None | None` or when we\0Aare unable to import the symbol `typing.Optional` and the Python version\0Ais 3.9 or below.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Typing documentation: Legal parameters for `Literal` at type check time](https://typing.python.org/en/latest/spec/literal.html#legal-parameters-for-literal-at-type-check-time)\0A", align 1
