@anon.bcaf8a6642ef434db28a340da408f3d7.2045 = private unnamed_addr constant [727 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `Unpack[]` on Python 3.11 and above, and suggests\0Ausing `*` instead.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A[PEP 646] introduced a new syntax for unpacking sequences based on the `*`\0Aoperator. This syntax is more concise and readable than the previous\0A`Unpack[]` syntax.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom typing import Unpack\0A\0A\0Adef foo(*args: Unpack[tuple[int, ...]]) -> None:\0A    pass\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Adef foo(*args: *tuple[int, ...]) -> None:\0A    pass\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as `Unpack[T]` and `*T` are considered\0Adifferent values when introspecting types at runtime. However, in most cases,\0Athe fix should be safe to apply.\0A\0A[PEP 646]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0646/\0A", align 1
